<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eleanor of Aquitaine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1122–<a href="/years/1204">1204</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramnulfids">Ramnulfids</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eleanor of Aquitaine was Duchess of Aquitaine and Countess of Poitou in her own right from 1137. She was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as wife of Louis VII, and after the annulment of that marriage she married Henry of Anjou in 1152, bringing Aquitaine into the Angevin Empire. She was the mother of the English kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> and John.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine.jpg" alt="Eleanor of Aquitaine" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Bishop · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_of_Fontevraud_Abbey_Eleanor_of_Aquitaine_effigy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eleanor was the eldest child of Duke William X of Aquitaine and Aénor de Châtellerault. She descended from a long line of dukes of Aquitaine going back to the 10th century, and twelfth-century Aquitaine was one of the wealthiest and most powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, whose rulers gave only nominal allegiance to the French crown. Her father set out on pilgrimage to the Shrine of Saint James of Compostela in 1137 and died on Good Friday, 9 April, leaving her as duchess.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 25 July 1137 Archbishop Geoffrey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> oversaw her marriage to Louis, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vi-of-france">Louis VI of France</a>. Her father-in-law died soon afterwards, making her queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. She accompanied Louis on the Second Crusade, which followed the fall of Edessa in December 1144. They had two daughters, Marie and Alix. After fifteen years of marriage, with no male heir, Pope Eugene III's earlier refusal of an annulment was reversed and the marriage was annulled in 1152 on grounds of consanguinity. Eleanor kept her lands, and in the same year she married Henry, Duke of Normandy.</p><p>Henry and Eleanor were crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on 19 December 1154, the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-plantagenet">House of Plantagenet</a>. They had five sons and three daughters but became estranged. Henry imprisoned her for supporting the revolt of 1173 by their sons Young Henry, Richard and Geoffrey, and she was not released until his death on 6 July 1189. As queen dowager she acted as regent for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> during his absences. When Richard died in 1199 she warned John to secure his possessions and campaigned against the claim of Arthur I of Brittany, and John was crowned on 27 May 1199.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Eleanor died at Fontevraud on 31 March or 1 April 1204 and was entombed in the abbey crypt between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> and Henry II. Her tomb has a painted stone effigy of her wearing a crown, with an open book in her hands. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> the abbey was sacked, and the human remains were scattered and have never been located. She had ten children and outlived eight of them.</p><p>Her descendants ruled various realms. Her grandchildren included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a>, Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengaria-of-castile">Berengaria of Castile</a>, Blanche of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Emperor Otto IV. No image of her dates from her lifetime, so the portraits and carvings attributed to her are speculative.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eleanor of Aquitaine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke William IX (1071–1126) is the earliest troubadour whose songs survive, eleven poems in Occitan that range from the bawdy to the courtly, and the courts of his son William X and his granddaughter <b>Eleanor of Aquitaine</b> were associated with poets such as Marcabru and Cercamon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Some women ruled in their own right: <b>Eleanor of Aquitaine</b> became duchess of Aquitaine at about fifteen and was queen of France and then of England.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li><li><blockquote>Archbishop Geoffrey of Bordeaux oversaw the wedding of <b>Eleanor of Aquitaine</b> and Prince Louis in the Cathedral of Saint-André in Bordeaux.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-25">25 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry of Anjou, the future Henry II of England, married <b>Eleanor of Aquitaine</b> after the annulment of her marriage to King Louis VII of France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-18">18 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry II inherited Anjou, Maine and Touraine from his father Geoffrey Plantagenet and Normandy through his mother Matilda, gained Aquitaine by marrying <b>Eleanor of Aquitaine</b> in 1152, and became king of England in 1154.</blockquote><a href="/state/angevin-empire">Angevin Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke William IX, the first known troubadour, wrote poetry in Occitan, and his granddaughter <b>Eleanor of Aquitaine</b> carried the county first to Louis VII of the Kingdom of France and then to Henry II of England.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-poitou">County of Poitou</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.58,0.34&amp;year=1175" aria-label="Poitiers on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4558 526 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Eleanor of Aquitaine"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5008 796h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5008 796h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.58,0.34&amp;year=1175">What was here in 1175? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
